    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A concrete driveway sealed properly can shrug off five or more winters of salt and slush. Sealed badly, it can look blotchy, turn white, or simply absorb water again by spring. The difference is almost never the brand of sealer. It is whether the slab was clean, whether it was truly dry, and whether the weather cooperated for a full day afterward. This plan puts those three things first.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="before-you-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Before You Start: Confirm the Driveway Needs It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pour a cup of water on a few spots: the tire paths, the apron near the garage, and a corner that rarely gets driven on. If the water darkens the concrete and soaks in within a few minutes, the driveway is absorbing and sealing will help. If it beads and sits for ten minutes or more, a previous sealer is still working, and you may only need to treat the worn areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the driveway's age. New concrete should usually cure about 28 days before a film sealer goes on. Many penetrating sealers allow earlier application, but only follow that if the label says so. For a driveway poured in late summer or fall, aim to seal before the first deicer, because the first winter is when a young slab is most vulnerable to scaling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick the sealer before you plan the work, because the product decides the tools and the timing. For a plain broom-finished driveway, a silane-siloxane penetrating sealer is the usual choice: no film, no slip, nothing to peel. A wet-look acrylic only makes sense for decorative concrete. Our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer reviews</a> compare specific products, and the broader <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> covers other slabs around the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look for problems a sealer cannot fix. Sunken panels, heaved sections, wide structural cracks and areas where the surface has already flaked away need repair or replacement first. Sealing over them only preserves the damage. If the driveway is sound apart from hairline cracks and dirty joints, you are ready to plan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Tools and Materials
          </h2>

          <p>
            You do not need specialized equipment for a typical driveway. A pressure washer, ideally with a rotary surface cleaner attachment, makes cleaning fast and even. A pump sprayer applies the sealer. A roller or a soft push broom spreads out puddles. Everything else is cleaning supplies and safety gear.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why you need it</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pressure washer and surface cleaner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Even, streak-free cleaning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,500 to 3,200 PSI is plenty for concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete degreaser</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lifts oil and grease spots</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scrub with a stiff nylon brush</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyurethane joint sealant and backer rod</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seals control joints and cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Self-leveling type for flat joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pump sprayer, 2 to 4 gallon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Applies sealer evenly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent-rated seals for solvent products</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Short-nap roller or push broom</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Back-rolls puddles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3/8 inch nap for film sealers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Painter's tape and plastic sheeting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Protects siding, doors and plants</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Also used for the moisture test</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gloves, eye protection, respirator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Safety</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Respirator rated for organic vapors with solvent products</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Buy enough sealer for the whole job plus 10 to 15 percent. Penetrating sealers commonly cover roughly 100 to 200 square feet per gallon per coat on broom-finished concrete, and most driveway treatments use two passes. Measure length times width, add the apron, walk and steps, and plan to the low end of the coverage range.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not own a pressure washer, renting one for a day is reasonable. A surface cleaner attachment is worth the extra cost because a wand alone tends to leave stripes that show through clear sealers. Our <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner guide</a> covers what to look for if you would rather buy one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps-1-3" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Steps 1-3: Clean, Repair and Dry
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clean every square foot</h3>

          <p>
            Start by sweeping off debris and moving cars, bins and planters. Treat oil spots with a concrete degreaser, letting it dwell for the time on the label and scrubbing with a nylon brush. Deep stains may need a poultice or a second round; our <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">guide to removing driveway oil stains</a> covers stubborn cases. Then pressure wash the whole slab in overlapping passes, working from the high end toward the street so dirty water drains away from cleaned areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat mildew and algae with a cleaner made for concrete before washing, especially on shaded edges. If the slab has white mineral deposits, called efflorescence, remove them with an efflorescence cleaner; sealing over them traps a white haze.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Repair joints and cracks</h3>

          <p>
            Clean out control joints and cracks wider than about an eighth of an inch. Insert foam backer rod in deep joints so the sealant is not wasted, then fill with a self-leveling polyurethane joint sealant. Hairline cracks can be left for the sealer; penetrating products soak into them. Let the joint sealant cure as directed before sealing the slab, and keep the sealer off fresh caulk unless the labels say they are compatible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let it dry completely</h3>

          <p>
            This is where most driveway jobs go wrong. Concrete holds water well after the surface looks dry. Give it at least 24 hours of dry weather after washing, and 48 hours if it is shaded, cool or humid. To check, tape a square of clear plastic to the slab for two to four hours. If moisture collects under the plastic or the concrete beneath looks darker, wait longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps-4-5" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Steps 4-5: Pick the Weather Window and Mask Off
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Check the forecast and slab temperature</h3>

          <p>
            Read the label for its temperature range. Many products call for air and surface temperatures between about 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, though some penetrating sealers allow application from about 40 degrees. You also need no rain for at least 12 to 24 hours after application, and no overnight frost. Early fall and late spring often offer the steadiest conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the slab, not just the air. A dark driveway in midday sun can run well above air temperature, which makes film sealers flash dry and bubble and can make penetrating sealers evaporate before they soak in. Plan to apply in the morning after the dew has dried, or later in the afternoon once the slab has cooled. Avoid windy days, which carry overspray onto cars and siding.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Protect everything you do not want sealed</h3>

          <p>
            Tape plastic over the bottom of garage doors, siding, brick, door thresholds and any painted surfaces near the edges. Cover plants and lawn edges with plastic or drop cloths, especially with solvent-based products. Move vehicles to the street and block the driveway with cones or tape so nobody drives onto it mid-job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan your route. Start at the far end near the garage and work toward the street so you never have to walk over fresh sealer. Divide the driveway into sections about the width you can comfortably reach, often 4 to 6 feet, and work across each section before moving on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps-6-7" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Steps 6-7: Apply the Sealer
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 6: Apply a penetrating sealer</h3>

          <p>
            Pour the sealer into a clean pump sprayer, pressurize it and spray in overlapping passes, keeping the tip a consistent distance from the surface. Most penetrating products call for a saturating coat, enough that the surface stays wet for a short time without puddling. Many labels then call for a second pass applied wet-on-wet, before the first coat dries. Others want separate coats with a drying period between them. Follow your label exactly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Puddles are the enemy. Low spots and the textured lows of broom finish collect sealer, and puddles left to dry can leave a shiny, sticky or blotchy residue. Keep a roller or a soft broom on hand and spread puddles into the surrounding concrete while they are still wet. Because penetrating sealers dry clear, note where you have been and keep a wet edge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 7: Or apply a film sealer in thin coats</h3>

          <p>
            Acrylic and urethane film sealers go on with a sprayer and back-roller or with a roller alone. The goal is thin, even coats. Many acrylics list roughly 150 to 300 square feet per gallon for the first coat. Apply the first coat, let it dry as the label directs, usually a few hours, then apply a second thin coat perpendicular to the first. Mix an anti-slip additive into the final coat on sloped driveways and walkways.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resist the urge to add extra product for more shine. Thick coats trap solvent and moisture, which causes white haze, bubbles and a soft film that marks under tires. If the first coat looks uneven, the second thin coat usually evens it out. For a deeper look at film versus penetrating choices, see our <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">penetrating sealer guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps-8-9" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Steps 8-9: Let It Cure and Check Your Work
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 8: Keep traffic off</h3>

          <p>
            Cure times vary. Many penetrating sealers allow foot traffic after a few hours and vehicles after about 24 hours. Film sealers often need 24 hours for foot traffic and 48 to 72 hours for cars, longer in cool or humid weather. Keep the driveway blocked off, and avoid turning the wheel while parked on a fresh film; it can scuff or lift the sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the weather during cure. If unexpected rain arrives soon after application, a penetrating sealer may lose some effectiveness and a film sealer may cloud. Our guide to <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">rain after driveway sealing</a> covers what to do in that case.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 9: Verify with a water test</h3>

          <p>
            A day or two after the sealer cures, sprinkle water across the driveway. On a penetrating-sealed slab, water should bead and the concrete should not darken. Any spot that darkens quickly was missed or under-applied; touch it up with another pass. Pay attention to edges, the ends of passes and the areas where you refilled the sprayer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Timelines From Labels</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24-48 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Drying after washing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">12-24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">No rain after application</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">about 24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Penetrating sealer before cars</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">48-72 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Film sealer before cars</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Write down the product, date, number of coats and how much you used. You will want that information when it is time to reseal, and it tells the next owner exactly what is on the slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Resealing a Driveway That Was Sealed Before
          </h2>

          <p>
            Find out what is already there. If water soaks into the concrete and the surface has no sheen, it is either bare or the old penetrating sealer has worn off; you can proceed as with a new application. If the surface has a sheen or film, it is probably acrylic or urethane, and the new sealer must be compatible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating over penetrating is straightforward: clean and reapply. Film over film works when the chemistry matches. Solvent acrylic bonds well over solvent acrylic, and water-based over water-based usually works after cleaning. Putting a solvent product over a water-based film can wrinkle or lift it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switching from film to penetrating requires stripping the film, because the penetrating sealer cannot reach the concrete through it. Chemical strippers made for acrylic sealers work but are messy and need careful rinsing. If the film is mostly worn away already, a thorough pressure wash may remove the rest; do a water test to confirm before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Often only part of the driveway needs work. Tire paths, the apron and the area where cars park wear out first. Spot-treating those areas with the same penetrating product extends the overall life without redoing the whole slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-or-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. DIY or Hire Out: Time, Cost and Judgment
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical two-car driveway takes a DIYer about one day of cleaning and repair, a day of drying, and two to three hours of application. Material costs for penetrating sealer commonly run from roughly one hundred to a few hundred dollars depending on the product, coats and driveway size, plus joint sealant and cleaning supplies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hiring a contractor costs more but saves the weekend and the equipment rental. Professional pricing varies widely by region and by what is included. Ask for a quote that names the exact sealer, the number of coats and the coverage rate, and whether cleaning, oil treatment and joint repair are included.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pro makes the most sense when the job is more than routine sealing: stripping an old film, extensive crack and joint repair, densifying a dusty slab before sealing, or a large or decorative driveway where mistakes are expensive. For a sound broom-finished driveway, DIY with a pump sprayer is well within most homeowners' abilities.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever route you take, the protection holds up best with simple habits: keep joints sealed, use deicers sparingly and avoid ammonium-based products, and test with water each fall. If you also have an asphalt section, note that it needs a different product and process; our <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">asphalt driveway sealing guide</a> covers that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the day after washing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete that looks dry on top can still hold water a little below the surface. Sealing too soon leaves penetrating products shallow and causes film sealers to cloud. Wait at least 24 hours of dry weather, longer in shade or cool temperatures, and confirm with a taped plastic sheet test.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working in midday sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hot slab makes sealer evaporate or flash dry before it soaks in or levels out. The result is bubbles, lap marks or weak protection. Seal in the morning after dew has dried or in late afternoon when the surface has cooled, and avoid windy days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting puddles dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer that pools in low spots or broom texture can dry into a shiny, sticky or blotchy patch. Spread puddles with a roller or soft broom while they are still wet, especially on penetrating products with higher solids.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the wrong sprayer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent-based sealers swell the rubber seals in common garden sprayers, causing leaks and drips within minutes. Use a sprayer with solvent-rated seals, typically Viton, for solvent products, and rinse any sprayer thoroughly after use according to the sealer's cleanup instructions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on it too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh film sealers can scuff, mark or lift under tires before they cure. Even penetrating sealers benefit from a full day before vehicles. Block the driveway with cones or tape and warn household members and delivery drivers ahead of time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to seal a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan for about two to three days start to finish. Cleaning and repair take most of a day, the slab needs 24 to 48 hours to dry, and application takes two to three hours for a typical two-car driveway. Then allow about a day before driving on penetrating sealers and two to three for film sealers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a sprayer or a roller?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For penetrating sealers, a pump sprayer is the best tool, with a roller or broom on hand to spread puddles. For film sealers, either a sprayer with back-rolling or a roller alone works; the roller helps keep coats thin and even. Solvent products need a sprayer with solvent-rated seals.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need two coats of sealer on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most penetrating sealers call for two passes, often applied wet-on-wet, to saturate the surface properly. Film sealers usually need two thin coats on bare concrete. When resealing a sound film, one thin coat may be enough. Always follow the specific product's directions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal my driveway in the fall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, early fall is often ideal, provided temperatures stay within the label range day and night and the slab can dry completely. Sealing before the first deicer use gives the driveway protection for winter. Avoid late fall when nights drop near freezing or dew lingers on the surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it rains after sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Light rain after a penetrating sealer has soaked in for several hours usually does little harm, though heavy rain soon after application can reduce effectiveness. Film sealers can cloud or wash out if rained on before curing. Do a water test after drying and touch up any weak areas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to fill cracks before sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fill control joints and cracks wider than about an eighth of an inch with a flexible polyurethane sealant before sealing. Open joints let water into the base, which leads to heaving and settling. Hairline cracks can be left; penetrating sealers soak into them and reduce water entry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I reseal a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating sealers commonly last five to ten years, with heavily salted, high-traffic driveways at the low end. Film sealers usually need recoating every two to four years. Use a water test each fall rather than a fixed schedule, and spot-treat high-wear areas when they start absorbing water.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

