        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sealing a concrete driveway comes down to nine steps, and the first five are preparation. Clean thoroughly, fill joints, let the slab dry completely, choose a mild and dry weather window, and protect everything nearby. Then apply a penetrating sealer in saturating passes, or a film sealer in thin coats, and keep traffic off until it cures.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Finish with a water test, touch up any spots that darken, and write down what you used. Repeat the water test every fall. With that routine, most concrete driveways need a full reseal only every several years, and they come through each winter in far better shape.
          </p>
